<edge source="us_china_competition::regulatory_intervention" target="ai_power_shift::model_deployment_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in regulatory actions (audits, forced rollbacks, stop-orders, heavy compliance requirements) causes firms to pause or slow rollouts while they redesign systems, obtain approvals, or incur compliance costs, directly reducing the rate of new model deployments.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_competition</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_competition::regulatory_intervention" target="middle_east_maritime::tanker_transit_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Intensified export controls, sanctions, or tighter port compliance measures tied to US–China competition increase legal and operational risks for shipowners and ports, prompting voyage cancellations and rerouting that reduce formal tanker transits through regional chokepoints.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_competition</data>
  <data key="d40">middle_east_maritime</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_competition::regulatory_intervention" target="north_korea_escalation::sanctions_pressure_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating export controls and secondary‑sanctions measures tied to US–China competition expand the scope and targeting of listings and prohibitions on firms and intermediaries, thereby raising cumulative sanctions pressure on networks connected to North Korea.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_competition</data>
  <data key="d40">north_korea_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_competition::regulatory_intervention" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">As competition drives tougher export controls, investment screening and administrative trade measures targeting China, governments institutionalize broader controls and secondary‑sanctions tools that are readily applied or adapted to restrict Russian trade and finance, raising effective sanctions pressure on Russia.</data>
